  block txt_sum_why: TextBlock {
    content: @md **What the difference does.** Every column shifts at once here, and Cohort Size shifts with them — which is the clearest sign that the second filter is selecting a different population rather than trimming a report.

**The second report describes a different population.** Filtering at the final-results level selects everyone who *ever* bought the category rather than those who bought it at acquisition, so Cohort Size itself moves: for Jan 2026 / Skin Care it reads 210 against 83. Month 0 is where it shows plainest — every acquired customer orders in their acquisition month by definition, so retention there is 100% when the filter profiles the cohort, and 44.8% when it only trims the output.;;
  }

  block txt_ov_hero: TextBlock {
    content: @md <div style="border-left:4px solid #05264C;padding:4px 0 4px 18px">
<h1 style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:26px;color:#05264C">The same cohort report, built two ways</h1>
<p style="margin:0;font-size:15px;color:#505157;line-height:1.6">
A <strong>cohort</strong> is a group of customers who started at the same time — everyone whose first purchase was in January is the January cohort. Every tab follows one month by month, <strong>twice</strong>: once modelled in the Holistics semantic layer, once as the SQL model an analyst would normally write.
</p>
</div>

**The two agree exactly — until someone asks a question the SQL was not written for.**;;
  }

  block txt_ov_numbers: TextBlock {
    content: @md ## What that looks like

January 2026, before and after filtering to skincare.

| | Semantic layer | SQL model |
|---|---|---|
| Cohort size | 381 | 381 |
| Month 0 · month 1 | 100.0% · 26.2% | 100.0% · 26.2% |
| Revenue, month 0 | $97,644 | $97,644 |
| **Filtered — cohort size** | **84** | **381** |
| **Filtered — month 0** | **100.0%** | **25.2%** |
| **Filtered — month 1** | **32.1%** | **6.6%** |

<div style="background:#F5F8FA;border-left:4px solid #1B7CE4;border-radius:6px;padding:14px 18px;margin-top:8px">
<strong style="color:#05264C">The top half is the point.</strong> Identical, to the dollar — the SQL build is neither careless nor wrong. It simply cannot answer a question it was not written for, and it fails quietly: cohort size stays at 381 while it counts only the customers who bought skincare. Month 0 is the tell. That is the month these customers joined, so retention is 100% by definition. 25.2% is not an approximation; it is incoherent.
</div>

Read the SQL column alone and you would stop the skincare campaign. Those customers did come back — they bought something else.;;
  }

  block txt_ov_right_card: TextBlock {
    content: @md <div style="background:#F5F8FA;border-left:4px solid #1B7CE4;border-radius:6px;padding:16px 18px;height:100%">
<div style="font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1B7CE4;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:8px">Semantic layer</div>
<div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#05264C;margin-bottom:12px">Skincare picks the group</div>
<p style="margin:0 0 12px;font-size:14px;color:#212327;line-height:1.6">
“Bought at acquisition” is modelled as an attribute of the <strong>customer</strong>, so the filter changes who is in the cohort. We then measure their whole life — the shampoo, the sofa, the phone case.
</p>
<div style="font-size:13px;color:#1F815A;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:10px">84 customers · 100% month 0 · 32.1% month 1</div>
<div style="font-size:12px;color:#505157;line-height:1.5;border-top:1px solid #E3E7ED;padding-top:10px">
Nothing was written in advance for skincare. The same holds for any category, product, country or basket size.
</div>
</div>;;
  }

  block txt_ov_wrong_card: TextBlock {
    content: @md <div style="background:#FFF7EA;border-left:4px solid #E36A01;border-radius:6px;padding:16px 18px;height:100%">
<div style="font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#B04A00;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:8px">SQL model</div>
<div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#05264C;margin-bottom:12px">Skincare slices the output</div>
<p style="margin:0 0 12px;font-size:14px;color:#212327;line-height:1.6">
The cohort was computed inside the query and frozen. Category is a <strong>column in the result</strong>, so filtering it cuts the finished table. It cannot reach back and change who the cohort is.
</p>
<div style="font-size:13px;color:#B04A00;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:10px">381 customers · 25.2% month 0 · 6.6% month 1</div>
<div style="font-size:12px;color:#505157;line-height:1.5;border-top:1px solid #E3E7ED;padding-top:10px">
To profile on something new, someone edits the query, re-runs it, and re-checks that it still ties out.
</div>
</div>;;
  }

  block txt_ov_skeptic: TextBlock {
    content: @md ## “We would parameterise the SQL”

Fair — and it works. It is also what one customer had built before moving to a semantic layer, so the cost is known rather than hypothetical:

- **“Nothing selected” stops being free.** A parameter always holds a value, so the query needs an `'All'` branch or it breaks when the filter is empty.
- **Every metric needs a twin.** A parameter cannot filter by itself, so each metric re-implements the test. That build ended with **seven `qualified_*` metrics** shadowing the seven real ones.
- **The value list is written by hand.** Six categories is fine. Products are **109 names across 4,543 IDs** — and a product launched this week is simply missing from the filter, with nobody told.

Each new dimension repeats all three. On the semantic side the filters read their own values from the data, an empty filter means no condition at all, and `LTV` is `LTV` whether or not a cohort filter is set.;;
  }

  block txt_ov_try: TextBlock {
    content: @md ## Try it

1. Open **Retention**. The two reports agree.
2. Set **Category** to `Skin Care`.
3. Watch the cohort size on the right refuse to move, and month 0 fall below 100%.

Filters are multi-select and they combine — add `Clothes`, then a **Product**, a **Country**, a **Units Band**. None of those combinations was declared in advance on the semantic side. Ask what each one would have cost on the other.;;
  }

